## Mechanical backstop

Both adapters (`.claude/hooks/check_commit_msg.py` and
`.agents/hooks/check_commit_msg.py`) symlink to the same shared
implementation, wired via each adapter's `commit-msg` hook, which itself
symlinks to `.git/hooks/commit-msg` — warn-only, exits 0 always, exempts
merges and `fixup!`/`squash!` commits. It runs two checks:

1. **Role-prefix check (all commits):** anything whose first line doesn't
   match `ROLE_PREFIX_RE` (Planner, Coder, Runner, Reviewer, Author,
   Researcher, Controller, Harness) is flagged, never rejected.
2. **Body check (every role commit that records directive work):** when the
   first line's role is one of `BODY_REQUIRED_ROLES` (Planner, Coder,
   Runner, Reviewer, Author, Researcher — `Harness:` is exempt, since a
   submodule-pointer bump serves no directive), the body (whole message,
   minus git's own trailing `#`-comment block) is checked for a directive
   reference and a tracker reference per "Attribution format" above. It
   covers all roles rather than only `Reviewer:` because each role now
   commits its own work. Missing either prints a WARN; it still never
   blocks — `commit-msg` is symlinked straight into `.git/hooks/commit-msg`,
   and a blocking check here would strand an agent mid-pass with no escape
   hatch.

## Durability note

<!-- SECTION:tracker_none:start -->
This project has no task tracker configured, so
`[SET AT SETUP: STATUS_HISTORY_PATH]` is deliberately kept in the project
repo rather than in `.friday/active/` — with no tracker, it is the ONLY
record of finished work that survives a fresh clone, so it must be tracked.
Setup refuses to run any configuration that would put it anywhere else (see
`check_status_history_not_gitignored()` in `init_harness.py`).

It is therefore a real source of truth here, and commits that change it
belong in the same role commit as the work they describe.
<!-- SECTION:tracker_none:end -->
<!-- SECTION:tracker_gitlab:start -->
`[SET AT SETUP: STATUS_HISTORY_PATH]` (like the rest of `.friday/active/`)
is tracked by **neither** the project repo nor `.friday` — a fresh clone of
either will never have it. Treat it as a convenience index, not a source of
truth: the genuinely durable carriers of a close-out are the **commit
message** (this file's format above) and the **tracker issue**, both of
which live in systems that survive a clone. A future reader reconstructing
history should follow commit hashes and tracker references, not
`status_history.md`.
